    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to establishing\nan electronic automated system for applications and waiting lists, and to\ndevelop a written procedure for applicant selection and rejection",
    "basePrintNo": "S6499",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6499-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §31, Priv Hous Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Private Housing Finance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6499\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the private housing finance law, in\nrelation to establishing an electronic automated system for applications\nand waiting lists, and to develop a written procedure for applicant\nselection and rejection\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo ensure that the process of choosing applicants for tenancy in Mitc-\nhell-Lama developments is consistent and made available to the public.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nNew subdivision 13 would allow for a commissioner or supervising agency\nto develop a written procedure with regard to how applications for\nadmission to a company are processed and numbered, and how tenants are\nselected.\n\nNew subdivision 14 would allow for the Commissioner of Homes and Commu-\nnity Renewal or Supervising Agency to develop a procedure where appli-\ncants are notified if their application is rejected. The notification\nwould be sent to the applicant in written form within sixty days of the\nlimited-profit housing company's decision to reject the applicant, and\nwould include reasons why the applicant was rejected and information\nregarding the appears process for such decision. Also, for purposes of\nthis subdivision, an applicant would not be deemed rejected if their\napplication is still active on the limited-profit housing company's\nwaiting list and such waiting list is still open and accepting applica-\ntions.\n\nThis bill would also allow for the Commissioner or the Supervising Agen-\ncy to develop and require the use of a publicly available electronic\nautomated system for limited-profit housing companies to store, process,\nand maintain applications and waiting lists.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development\n(HPD) is the nation's largest municipal housing preservation and devel-\nopment agency. Its mission is to make strategic investments that will\nimprove and strengthen neighborhoods while preserving the stability and\naffordability of existing housing stock. The Mitchell-Lama program was\ncreated in 1955 to provide affordable rental and cooperative housing to\nmoderate-income families. In New York City, there are 97 HPD-supervised\nMitchell-Lama rental and limited-equity cooperative developments, with\nmore than 45,300 total units. Each development is administered by either\na Board of Directors (cooperatives) or a managing agent (rentals and\ncooperatives).\n\nApplicants apply to the individual developments for a unit of a specific\nsize, and their names are placed on a manually maintained waiting list\n(list) created by HPD staff and subsequently maintained by the respec-\ntive managing agents. As vacancies occur, Applicants should be selected\nin the order they appear on the list and sent to HPD for approval.\n\nAn audit completed in July 2015 found the following inconstancies with\nthe application process: applicants who were next in line for units in a\nspecific development were not always offered and/or awarded a unit in\nthe order they were listed. Also, they identified unit occupants whose\nnames were not submitted to HPD for approval by the respective managing\nagents. The audit also found inconsistencies in the methods used by HPD\nstaff to place applicants on development waiting lists. This bill would\nhelp ensure that any applicant to a Mitchell-Lama housing unit is aware\nof their status on the waiting list, if applicable, and would also\nrequire all Mitchell-Lama housing developments to have a uniform, base-\nline standard for the handling and processing of applications. By adding\nthese new requirements, we hope to rectify these issues and streamline\nthe application process.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS 6264 COMRIE Same as A 4504 Blake.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect sixty days after it shall become law.\n",
